  "answer": "To understand anaerobic respiration in the school laboratory, you can follow a procedure that involves testing for the production of carbon dioxide and heat. First, you need to prepare a glucose solution and remove the dissolved oxygen from it by heating and cooling the solution without shaking. Then, add yeast to the solution. To prevent oxygen from entering, pour a layer of liquid paraffin on top of the mixture. This creates an anaerobic environment. Next, attach a tube to the mixture leading to a bicarbonate/indicator solution (like lime water) to detect the release of carbon dioxide, which will turn the lime water milky. Additionally, use a thermometer to observe any change in temperature, indicating the release of heat during the process. This experiment helps demonstrate how anaerobic respiration produces carbon dioxide and heat without the presence of oxygen.",
